* ============================================================================
* workspace_writeback.dts -- the return leg: disk -> memo -> RAM -> DISK.
*
* WORKSPACE WRITEBACK (owner-ruled verb, 2026-08-12) writes every table the
* POSTURE declares, plus each area's memo sidecar, out of wherever they
* currently live and onto a real disk root. Hydration is its proven inverse,
* so the round trip is the test: bytes that went into a memo, through the RAM
* VFS, and back to disk must still read as the same rows.
*
* WHAT THIS SPEC GUARDS, beyond "it wrote some files":
*
*   The manifest, not the loop. Enumeration comes from the posture's AREA
*   lines -- the record of what the workspace IS -- not from the session's
*   attached order. The first cut asked the session and silently wrote 15 of
*   27 files, reporting cheerful success; the same order-dependent
*   enumeration is why a posture once omitted students.cdx. A count is a fact
*   about a loop until something declares what the count SHOULD be.
*
*   The refusal is a feature. A partial workspace on disk looks finished, so
*   a shortfall ABORTS. WB_T5 is the arm that proves the refusal still
*   refuses -- a regression that silently started succeeding here would be
*   worse than one that started failing.
*
*   Nothing means nothing. An aborted writeback must leave the filesystem
*   untouched, empty directories included (measured: the first cut created
*   the target dirs before the manifest check while claiming it had not).
*
* Markers:
*   WB_T1  round trip preserves VALUES (Taylor Quinn from disk->memo->RAM->disk)
*   WB_T2  round trip preserves COUNT (GO 200 reads Avery Davis -- the count
*          asserted by REACHABILITY and a field value, not by a counter:
*          no RECCOUNT() exists in the SYSFUNC catalog, and a count is a
*          fact about a loop anyway; record 200 reading as record 200's
*          VALUE is the by-value form of "all 200 made the trip")
*   WB_T3  the memo sidecar made the trip (NOTES readable, null-virgin)
*   WB_T4  a second table proves it was not a one-table accident
*   WB_T5  ABORT arm: one table open, 13-table posture -> refuses
*   WB_T6  the aborted target does not exist afterward (nothing means nothing)
*   WB_G1  fixture guard: the sentinel was actually planted
*   WB_T7  COLLISION arm: writeback into an existing target without CONFIRM
*          refuses and WRITES NOTHING -- proven by the sentinel surviving
*   WB_T8  CONFIRM arm: the same call WITH CONFIRM does replace
*   WB_T9  the .__wbak backup holds the PRIOR content and OPENS as a table --
*          the undo of last resort, proven recoverable rather than asserted
*   WB_T10 WITH INDEXES lands the index container bytes
*   WB_T11 and the mirrored container WORKS after the documented destination
*          rebuild (BUILDLMDB), so the mirror is functional, not file-shaped
*
* WHY THE SENTINEL (WB_G1..WB_T9): writeback writes the SAME bytes every run,
* so "replaced" and "refused" are indistinguishable by inspection -- both leave
* a target full of correct-looking data. Mutating the landed copy first makes
* the two outcomes produce DIFFERENT VALUES, so each arm reads a field instead
* of trusting a message. Added 2026-08-12 after these three branches were found
* never to have executed: a destructive verb had its refusal proven and its
* replacement path unproven.
*
* WHAT WB_T11 DOES NOT CLAIM: the landed .cdx is a BYTE-MIRROR, not a ready
* index. LMDB environments are not carried (owner rule, lmdb only for disks),
* so the destination needs the rebuild this arm performs. That is the promise
* WITH INDEXES actually makes; the arm asserts exactly it and nothing more.
*
* Assertion vocabulary note (2026-08-12): WB_T5/WB_T6 use FILE(<path>),
* added to the engine for exactly these arms -- an absence proof needs a
* by-value read of the filesystem. FILE() is deliberately broader than
* VFP's (any entry counts, directories included) because "nothing means
* nothing" fails on a leftover empty directory too.
*
* PATH RESOLUTION, and why this spec could not be trusted before 2026-08-12:
* every relative path below -- the WRITEBACK TO target, the ERASE DIR
* bootstrap/teardown, the FILE() probes, and SET PATH DBF -- must name ONE
* directory. They did not. WRITEBACK, ERASE DIR and FILE() resolved against
* the process CWD while SET PATH resolved against DATA, so this spec wrote to
* one place and read from another. It agreed only because datarun.ps1 runs
* with cwd = DATA, which made the two spellings coincide by accident.
* Consequence worth remembering: a Linux run of this spec at a different cwd
* reported SIX GREEN MARKERS while reading a directory populated by an
* earlier, unrelated run -- a false green produced by the very spec written to
* hunt them. All four surfaces now route through paths::resolve_in_slot, so a
* relative token means the same directory to the writer, the reader, the
* prober and the teardown, whatever the shell's cwd.
*
* Indexes are deliberately NOT asserted: each table's index CHOICE travels in
* the posture (index=/indextype=, mixed types preserved) and the FILE is
* rebuildable at the destination. WITH INDEXES copies container bytes when a
* byte-mirror is wanted; that arm is not covered here.
*
* Disposable: writes to dbf\wbregress; both dbf\wbregress and dbf\wbabort are
* ERASE DIR'd at bootstrap AND teardown, so every run is clean-slate. The
* pre-clean is not hygiene theater: a leftover wbregress makes WRITEBACK
* refuse on collision while the reads below happily consume the PREVIOUS
* run's files -- a stale false green, the worst kind.
* Requires the mcc_minidb_memo catalog row (written by mcc_add_notes_memo).
*
* Run: DO workspace_writeback | regression: REGRESSION RUN WORKSPACE_WRITEBACK
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* --- bootstrap: clean slate (rerun hygiene) ---------------------------------
ERASE DIR DBF/wbregress CONFIRM
ERASE DIR DBF/wbabort CONFIRM

FORMULA "WS-WRITEBACK-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --- the round trip: memo -> RAM -> disk ------------------------------------
DO mem
WORKSPACE LOAD mcc_minidb_memo MEMO RAM
WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mcc_minidb_memo TO DBF/wbregress
WORKSPACE CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT

* --- read the landed copy: it must be the SAME DATABASE ---------------------
SET PATH DBF DBF/wbregress
SET PATH INDEXES DBF/wbregress
SELECT 1
USE STUDENTS NOINDEX
GO 1
? "WB_T1_values_survive_round_trip:" + (ALLTRIM(FNAME) = "Quinn")
* The count, read by value: GO 200 only lands if all 200 records made the
* trip, and the landed record must read as the SOURCE's record 200 (Avery
* Davis). A short table either fails the GO or lands on the wrong name --
* both red. (Fixture ground truth: x64 STUDENTS r1 = Quinn Taylor,
* r200 = Avery Davis, SID 50000199.)
GO 200
? "WB_T2_count_survives:" + (ALLTRIM(FNAME) = "Avery")
GO 1
? "WB_T3_memo_sidecar_made_the_trip:" + (NOTES = "")
CLOSE

SELECT 2
USE TEACHERS NOINDEX
GO 1
? "WB_T4_second_table_intact:" + (LNAME > "")
CLOSE

* --- the replacement cycle: refusal, CONFIRM, and the .__wbak undo ----------
* Plant a sentinel in the LANDED copy so the next two outcomes differ by value.
SELECT 1
USE STUDENTS NOINDEX
GO 1
REPLACE LNAME WITH "WBAKSENTINEL"
CLOSE
USE STUDENTS NOINDEX
GO 1
? "WB_G1_sentinel_planted:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "WBAKSENTINEL")
CLOSE

* Same writeback, same target, NO CONFIRM -- must refuse and write nothing.
DO mem
WORKSPACE LOAD mcc_minidb_memo MEMO RAM
WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mcc_minidb_memo TO DBF/wbregress
WORKSPACE CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT
SET PATH DBF DBF/wbregress
SELECT 1
USE STUDENTS NOINDEX
GO 1
? "WB_T7_refusal_wrote_nothing:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "WBAKSENTINEL")
CLOSE

* Now WITH CONFIRM -- must replace, and must leave the prior bytes in .__wbak.
DO mem
WORKSPACE LOAD mcc_minidb_memo MEMO RAM
WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mcc_minidb_memo TO DBF/wbregress CONFIRM
WORKSPACE CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT
SET PATH DBF DBF/wbregress
SELECT 1
USE STUDENTS NOINDEX
GO 1
? "WB_T8_confirm_replaced:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "Taylor")
CLOSE
USE STUDENTS.__wbak.dbf NOINDEX
GO 1
? "WB_T9_wbak_holds_prior:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "WBAKSENTINEL")
CLOSE

* --- WITH INDEXES: the byte-mirror arm --------------------------------------
ERASE DIR DBF/wbidx CONFIRM
DO mem
WORKSPACE LOAD mcc_minidb_memo MEMO RAM
WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mcc_minidb_memo TO DBF/wbidx WITH INDEXES
WORKSPACE CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT
? "WB_T10_index_container_landed:" + FILE("DBF/wbidx/indexes/STUDENTS.cdx")
SET PATH DBF DBF/wbidx
SET PATH INDEXES DBF/wbidx/indexes
SET PATH LMDB DBF/wbidx/lmdb
SELECT 1
USE STUDENTS
BUILDLMDB
SET ORDER TO TAG LNAME
GO TOP
? "WB_T11_mirror_usable_after_rebuild:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "Anderson")
CLOSE

* --- the abort arm: one table open against a 13-table posture ---------------
WORKSPACE CLOSE
DO x64
USE STUDENTS NOINDEX
WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mcc_minidb_memo TO DBF/wbabort
CLOSE
* If the refusal held, nothing was created. FILE() on a member that WOULD
* have landed is the by-value read of "the filesystem is untouched".
? "WB_T5_shortfall_refused:" + (FILE("DBF/wbabort/STUDENTS.dbf") = .F.)
? "WB_T6_aborted_target_absent:" + (FILE("DBF/wbabort/BUILDING.dbf") = .F.)

FORMULA "WS-WRITEBACK-PROOF-END"

* --- teardown: nothing left on disk ------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE CLOSE
ERASE DIR DBF/wbregress CONFIRM
ERASE DIR DBF/wbabort CONFIRM
ERASE DIR DBF/wbidx CONFIRM
DO x64
